{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T2KbA1qTpdDioBBiaY4MpR","type":"tool_result","content":"0\tid: srd002-sys\n1\ttitle: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n2\t\n3\tproblem: |\n4\t  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n5\t  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n6\t  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n7\t  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n8\t  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n9\t  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n10\t  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n11\t  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n12\t\n13\t  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n14\t  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n15\t  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n16\t\n17\tgoals:\n18\t  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query and SIGPIPE handler that work on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n19\t  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n20\t  - G3: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n21\t\n22\trequirements:\n23\t  R1:\n24\t    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n25\t    items:\n26\t      - R1.1:\n27\t          text: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n28\t      - R1.2:\n29\t          text: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n30\t      - R1.3:\n31\t          text: |\n32\t            Must provide func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file\n33\t            descriptor refers to a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: call\n34\t            golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return\n35\t            err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors.\n36\t            The caller (pkg/format ColorEnabled) is responsible for extracting the fd from an\n37\t            io.Writer via type assertion to *os.File. Required by pkg/format to suppress color\n38\t            output when stdout is redirected.\n39\t      - R1.4:\n40\t          text: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n41\t      - R1.5:\n42\t          text: |\n43\t            Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the\n44\t            process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer.\n45\t            Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of\n46\t            size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not\n47\t            use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching\n48\t            GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n49\t      - R1.6:\n50\t          text: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n51\t      - R1.7:\n52\t          text: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n53\t\n54\t  R2:\n55\t    title: Extended file metadata\n56\t    items:\n57\t      - R2.1:\n58\t          text: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n59\t      - R2.2:\n60\t          text: |\n61\t            FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n62\t\n63\t              type FileInfo struct {\n64\t                  Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n65\t                  Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n66\t                  Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n67\t                  Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n68\t                  Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n69\t                  Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n70\t                  Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n71\t                  Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n72\t                  Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n73\t                  Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n74\t                  ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n75\t                  Info    os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n76\t              }\n77\t      - R2.3:\n78\t          text: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n79\t      - R2.4:\n80\t          text: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n81\t      - R2.5:\n82\t          text: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n83\t      - R2.6:\n84\t          text: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n85\t\n86\t  R3:\n87\t    title: SIGWINCH handling\n88\t    items:\n89\t      - R3.1:\n90\t          text: |\n91\t            Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n92\t      - R3.2:\n93\t          text: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n94\t      - R3.3:\n95\t          text: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n96\t\n97\tnon_goals:\n98\t  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n99\t  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ed0f51a-51be-49ea-a57b-d29c31e18a63","uuid":"ed06eac2-7426-4403-b31a-03817eeb2ce6","timestamp":"2026-04-19T16:59:57.736Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5010/docs/specs/software-requirements/srd002-sys.yaml","content":"id: srd002-sys\ntitle: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n\n  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query and SIGPIPE handler that work on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n  - G3: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n    items:\n      - R1.1:\n          text: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n      - R1.2:\n          text: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n      - R1.3:\n          text: |\n            Must provide func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file\n            descriptor refers to a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: call\n            golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return\n            err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors.\n            The caller (pkg/format ColorEnabled) is responsible for extracting the fd from an\n            io.Writer via type assertion to *os.File. Required by pkg/format to suppress color\n            output when stdout is redirected.\n      - R1.4:\n          text: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n      - R1.5:\n          text: |\n            Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the\n            process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer.\n            Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of\n            size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not\n            use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching\n            GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n      - R1.6:\n          text: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n      - R1.7:\n          text: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n\n  R2:\n    title: Extended file metadata\n    items:\n      - R2.1:\n          text: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n      - R2.2:\n          text: |\n            FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n\n              type FileInfo struct {\n                  Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n                  Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n                  Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n                  Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n                  Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n                  Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n                  Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n                  Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n                  Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n                  Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n                  ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n                  Info    os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n              }\n      - R2.3:\n          text: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n      - R2.4:\n          text: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n      - R2.5:\n          text: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n      - R2.6:\n          text: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n\n  R3:\n    title: SIGWINCH handling\n    items:\n      - R3.1:\n          text: |\n            Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n      - R3.2:\n          text: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n      - R3.3:\n          text: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n\nnon_goals:\n  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces.","numLines":100,"startLine":0,"totalLines":196}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WJp7PCvg3ZLzHgaqgystLE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have full context. Let me implement the changes to `pkg/sys/terminal.go`.\n\nThe current `IsTerminal` uses `IoctlGetWinsize`. Per R1.3 in the SRD, it should actually use `IoctlGetWinsize` (not `IoctlGetTermios` as D1 suggests — the SRD is authoritative). The SRD says: \"call golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return err == nil.\"\n\nThe current implementation already does this correctly. The main change needed per R4 is to have `TerminalWidth` use `IsTerminal` as a guard before the ioctl.\n\nLet me update `terminal.go`:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2348,"cache_read_input_tokens":72813,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2348},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ed0f51a-51be-49ea-a57b-d29c31e18a63","uuid":"f50f5eff-8318-4222-9115-4e4bd1634656"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WJp7PCvg3ZLzHgaqgystLE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01E1bV6s2XKa3EfncdqEwx5G","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5010/pkg/sys/terminal.go","content":"// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n\npackage sys\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\n\t\"golang.org/x/sys/unix\"\n)\n\n// stdoutFD is the file descriptor for stdout, used by TerminalWidth.\nconst stdoutFD = 1\n\n// IsTerminal reports whether fd refers to a terminal device.\n// Returns false for pipes, regular files, non-terminal descriptors,\n// and invalid file descriptors without panicking.\n//\n// R1.3: terminal detection via IoctlGetWinsize.\n// R2.2-R2.4: works for any fd (stdin=0, stdout=1, stderr=2, or other).\nfunc IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool {\n\t_, err := unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ)\n\treturn err == nil\n}\n\n// TerminalWidth returns the width of the terminal in columns by querying\n// stdout via the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl. Returns an error if stdout is not a\n// terminal or the ioctl fails.\n//\n// R1.1: terminal width query via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl.\n// R2.5: uses IsTerminal as a guard before attempting the ioctl.\n// D2: no fallback width; returns 0 and the error on failure.\nfunc TerminalWidth() (int, error) {\n\tif !IsTerminal(stdoutFD) {\n\t\treturn 0, fmt.Errorf(\"stdout (fd %d) is not a terminal\", stdoutFD)\n\t}\n\tws, err := unix.IoctlGetWinsize(stdoutFD, unix.TIOCGWINSZ)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn 0, err\n\t}\n\treturn int(ws.Col), nil\n}\n\n// OnTerminalResize registers a callback invoked when the terminal is resized\n// (SIGWINCH). The callback receives the new terminal width.\n//\n// R3.1: SIGWINCH-based resize notification.\nfunc OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) {\n}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2348,"cache_read_input_tokens":72813,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2348},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5ed0f51a-51be-49ea-a57b-d29c31e18a63","uuid":"2d8eb881-4fa6-4227-ba57-b03e627e728e"}
